# Experiment 15: the powder brief, answered in 1890.
#
# `19-waterline` ends with a note that reads, in full:
#
#   The pulley is the one first-era component this design does not use, and the
#   reason is worth writing down: a pulley is a *ratio*, and a river turning at
#   speed 1 already suits a crusher, which takes 2 or less. Ask the same era for
#   the `crush` brief -- which needs a mill, and a mill wants speed 4 -- and the
#   pulley train stops being optional.
#
# This is that design, and it exists because the world outside it changed rather
# than because the machine did. The Mining Valley sells *powder*; a mill is the
# only component that makes any; a mill will not turn below speed 4; and there
# is no grid in 1890 to put a motor on.
#
#   river -> wheel -> belt -> crusher          at the speed the river turns
#         -> wheel -> belt -> pulley -> mill   geared up four times
#
# Three mechanical facts, none of them cosmetic.
#
# 1. A wheel turns at speed 1 and a mill wants 4, so the pulley is geared up
#    four times. A pulley grips at 90 rotary a tick and loses 6% of whatever it
#    does pass, so two wheels go in to get one mill turning, and the surplus is
#    not headroom -- it is the belt slipping, and it leaves through the waste
#    column every tick. That is the first era's ceiling, and it is why a
#    water-driven plant is a row of small machines rather than one large one.
#
# 2. The pulley is on cast iron and the wheels are not. A mill shakes at 5,
#    timber rates 4, and the pulley is the one thing rigidly bolted to the mill
#    -- so either the frame changes or that wire becomes a second belt and pays
#    a second 6%. The frame is the cheaper answer here precisely because the
#    pulley is already a belt. There is no third answer: steel rates 9 and this
#    valley cannot make any for another century and a half.
#
# 3. Everything else the wheels drive is on a belt, which is what keeps them on
#    timber at all. Take the word off the four drive wires and the crushers pull
#    their own wheels apart, exactly as they do in `19-waterline`.
#
# What it costs, against `18-powderline` -- the same ore, the same mill, on a
# grid:
#
#                    this        18-powderline
#   powder        90.00/tick     67.50/tick
#   water            727/tick             0
#   grid                   0        139 MW
#   tiles                286           204
#   drive    4 wheels, 1 pulley    3 motors, 2 gearboxes
#
# Which is the Mining Valley's whole argument in one table, and note which way
# the first line points. The first era is not worse. It is *larger and thirstier
# and free*, and it does not wait a century and a half for a grid.

machine "Water Powder Line"
brief line

# ---- the race: one pump per wheel, because a wheel takes 200 water a tick
pump      P1  at 0,0    draws water
pump      P2  at 0,5    draws water
pump      P3  at 13,5   draws water
pump      P4  at 19,5   draws water

waterwheel W1 at 3,0
waterwheel W2 at 3,5
waterwheel W3 at 13,0
waterwheel W4 at 18,0

# ---- the ore path: lump in, crushed twice, milled to powder
inlet     I1  at 0,10   draws ore
crusher   C1  at 3,10   frame iron
crusher   C2  at 7,10   frame iron
mill      MI1 at 11,10  frame iron
outlet    O1  at 15,10

# ---- the drive train the mill needed, and the only iron in the race
pulley    PL1 at 16,5   ratio -4  frame iron

wire P1.water -> W1.water
wire P2.water -> W2.water
wire P3.water -> W3.water
wire P4.water -> W4.water

# The crushers: one wheel each, slackly, at the speed the river turns.
wire W1.rotary -> C1.drive  belt
wire W2.rotary -> C2.drive  belt

# The mill: two wheels into one pulley, geared up four times. The wires in are
# belts because the wheels are timber; the wire out is not, because a pulley is
# already a belt and paying for two of them buys nothing.
wire W3.rotary -> PL1.in  belt
wire W4.rotary -> PL1.in  belt
wire PL1.out -> MI1.drive

wire I1.out -> C1.in
wire C1.out -> C2.in
wire C2.out -> MI1.in
wire MI1.out -> O1.solid
